## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design comprehensive performance tests for multi-stage pipelines under bursty traffic, focusing on latency distribution, autoscaling behavior, backpressure mechanisms, and failure prevention strategies.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency measurement with p50/p95/p99 percentiles
- Bounded queues and backpressure for flow control
- Circuit breakers and fail-fast paths for failure isolation
- Autoscaling responsiveness and resource constraint management
- Distributed tracing for per-stage telemetry and bottleneck identification

## Code Example
```java
// Load profile configuration
public class LoadProfile {
    private final int baseThroughput;
    private final int burstMultiplier;
    private final Duration burstDuration;
    private final List<FrameSize> frameSizes;

    public static LoadProfile videoProcessingTest() {
        return new LoadProfile(1000, 5, Duration.ofMinutes(20),
            Arrays.asList(FrameSize.SMALL, FrameSize.MEDIUM, FrameSize.LARGE));
    }
}

// Performance test implementation
public class PipelinePerformanceTest {

    @Test
    public void testEndToEndLatency() {
        LoadProfile profile = LoadProfile.videoProcessingTest();
        MetricsCollector collector = new MetricsCollector();

        // Generate bursty traffic
        CompletableFuture<Void> test = CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            generateTraffic(profile, collector);
        });

        // Monitor autoscaler responsiveness
        monitorAutoscaling(collector);

        // Validate SLA adherence
        test.thenRun(() -> validateSLAs(collector));
    }

    private void validateSLAs(MetricsCollector collector) {
        LatencyMetrics latency = collector.getLatencyMetrics();
        assertTrue(latency.getP95() < Duration.ofSeconds(10));
        assertTrue(latency.getP99() < Duration.ofSeconds(15));

        AutoscalingMetrics autoscaling = collector.getAutoscalingMetrics();
        assertTrue(autoscaling.getScaleUpTime() < Duration.ofMinutes(2));
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time presence is sensitive to tail latency and backpressure. This question probes how an interviewer evaluates scalable WebSocket gateways, heartbeat resilience, and autoscaling behavior under bursty traffic, mirroring production issues at large-scale messaging apps.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket scale and connection churn
- Backpressure propagation across gateway and bus
- Heartbeat/timestamp accuracy and liveness tuning
- Autoscaling strategies (Kubernetes/HPA, event-driven scaling)
- Test harnesses: k6/ws, Gatling, Locust

## Code Example
```
import ws from 'k6/ws';
export const options = {vus: 1000, duration: '2m'};
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://presence.example';
  ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'presence', status:'online'}));
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate latency components (gateway vs. downstream services)?
- How would you validate a canary rollout maintains SLAs under burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical understanding of GraphQL-specific performance issues, notably nested resolvers and potential N+1 queries, in a beginner-friendly, repeatable way.

## Key Concepts
- GraphQL resolver timings
- N+1 query patterns and detection
- Field-level instrumentation (Apollo tracing)
- Load testing patterns: ramp and spike
- Layer isolation with mocks/replayed traces

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: k6 load test for GraphQL
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ query: "{ user(id:1){ id name posts { id title } } }" });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/graphql', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect N+1 without changing application code?
- What instrumentation would you add if you could only modify test data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for real-time collaboration reveal tail latency, backpressure handling, and cross-region performance, crucial for high-scale video platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency across microservices
- End-to-end tracing and replay
- Backpressure and autoscaling
- Network emulation across mobile/desktop

## Code Example
```javascript
// k6 load test example
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep, check } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '2m', target: 0 }
  ],
  thresholds: { 'http_req_duration': ['p(95)<500'] }
};
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ userId: __VU, stroke: { id: Math.random().toString(36).slice(2), x: Math.random(), y: Math.random(), color: '#0af' } });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/stroke', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(0.1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you instrument end-to-end latency and replay traces to identify bottlenecks?
- How would you validate backpressure strategies when bursts scale beyond provisioned capacity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world concurrency and multi-region resilience for a streaming AI workload beyond typical batch jobs.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency, autoscaling, per-tenant QoS, cache effectiveness, end-to-end tracing
- Backpressure handling, failure injection, graceful degradation
- Serverless vs. reserved compute trade-offs in a streaming path

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://transcription.example.com/stream';
  const s = ws.connect(url, {}, (socket) => {
    socket.on('open', () => socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'start'})));
    socket.on('message', (msg) => {});
  });
  check(s, { 'connected': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model per-tenant QoS and quotas in your workload generators?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use to trigger autoscaling up and down?

